00:07:06.982 --> 00:07:08.690
And so you actually
get to take advantage

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:12.740
of this with no change
in price or anything.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.630
So this is actually
super exciting.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:18.022
So we didn't just
make Filestore fast,

00:07:18.022 --> 00:07:19.730
we actually made it
really easy to reason

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.680
about the performance you get.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.880
If you're price
sensitive, pick standard.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.080
In which case, your
performance varies a little bit

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.850
based on capacity.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:34.935
If you are performance
sensitive, pick premium.

00:07:34.935 --> 00:07:37.060
Your performance doesn't
vary no matter what scale,

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:38.980
and you're not paying per IOP.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:41.030
You're just paying
dollars per gig per month.

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:42.880
And it's a fixed flat fee.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.562
And in fact, you
preprovision your capacity.

00:07:45.562 --> 00:07:47.020
And so you know
right up front, I'm

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:50.135
going to spend this
many dollars on storage.

00:07:50.135 --> 00:07:52.510
And so it's actually pretty
cool in terms of your pricing

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:57.330
and predicting your
pricing, it's great.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.533
Let's talk quickly
about features.

00:07:59.533 --> 00:08:01.200
This is kind of an
eye chart, so I'm not

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.760
going to spend a
lot of time here.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:06.220
But at a high level, you
can configure shares up

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:07.510
to about 64 terabytes.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:10.450
Premium and standard have
different minimum sizes,

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.345
so that we can guarantee
the performance.

00:08:12.345 --> 00:08:13.720
One of the really
exciting things

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:16.750
is because you have to
preprovision your size,

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:18.250
not only do you
know how much you're

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:20.060
going to pay at the
end of the month,

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:22.300
but you can actually
grow it online.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.987
And we have Stackdriver
metrics and alerts,

00:08:24.987 --> 00:08:27.320
which can automatically fire
and do things like webhooks

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:30.340
to automatically grow the size
should you want to do that.

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:33.370
So you can get a
page if you're going

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:37.600
to exceed the capacity you think
you should pay for as well.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.059
We are at about 500 active
clients out of about 5,000

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:41.559
connected.

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:45.730
That's kind of a small render
farm or a small batch workload.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:50.180
Or even maybe a
midsize batch workload.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:53.280
We're a zonal service
with high availability,

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.570
with three-9's SLA.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.460
And typical recovery
time when we

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:00.910
need to reboot for software
upgrade or we detect an issue

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.070
is typically under
120 seconds, which

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:06.070
is within the NFS timeout.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:09.640
From a networking perspective,
everything is super secure.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:13.030
We're RFC1918
private IP addresses.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:17.590
And you can access it
cross-zone and cross-region,

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.570
but not from the
public internet.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:22.960
Anyone ever put an NFS server
on the public internet?

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:24.370
I've done it.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.360
Don't do it.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.140
It's not a good idea.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.440
So you can't even do
that if you wanted to

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.020
in Google Cloud, which is great.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:34.340
And like all Google
products, we're super secure.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:36.410
We think security
is really important.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.577
And so we have encryption
at rest and in transit,

00:09:38.577 --> 00:09:40.160
according to the
rest of the platform.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.035
So there's some details
and some white papers

00:09:42.035 --> 00:09:44.720
that we've released there.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.920
Integrations-- we are integrated
with a lot of the main things

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:52.610
that you're going to use
in GCP, like particularly

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.548
Compute Engine, GCE, and
Kubernetes Engine, GKE.

00:09:55.548 --> 00:09:57.590
On Compute Engine, I
especially want to call out,

00:09:57.590 --> 00:09:59.840
we're integrated not
only with regular VMs,

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.060
but also preemptable VMs.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:06.020
So this is really great for
your batch compute workloads.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:08.720
And Kubernetes
Engine, in GKE, we're

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.158
integrated with the CSI driver.

00:10:10.158 --> 00:10:12.450
And so you can actually
dynamically provision Filestore

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:13.460
instances.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.590
Speaking about
dynamically provisioning,

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:17.768
there's two ways
to do that as well.

00:10:17.768 --> 00:10:18.560
You can go to the--

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:22.280
I mean, you can go to the
console, which is the GUI.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.745
You can do it via API, or
you can use our command line.

00:10:24.745 --> 00:10:26.870
But you can also use it
through Deployment Manager,

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:29.900
which is our native
offering, and also Terraform.

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:33.050
I think the Terraform team tells
me something like 10% or 15%

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:36.290
of Filestore configurations
are done through Terraform,

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:38.100
in fact, which is pretty cool.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:44.840
So we worked with the HashiCorp
to put our integration

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.880
in there.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:47.870
Cool.

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:50.910
Now, we're a storage
product, and so backups--

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:53.450
we're more traditional,
rather than object storage,

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:55.190
which is cloud native.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.260
So backups are a thing.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:00.200
We're integrated with both
Actifio and Commvault,

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.350
which are two of the leading
third party backup vendors.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:05.060
Anyone using any of these--

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.500
Actifio or Commvault already?

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:07.860
Yeah, these are pretty common.

