00:11:58.142 --> 00:12:00.710
journey from
completely on premise

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:04.225
into completely cloud-based
development and test.

00:12:04.225 --> 00:12:06.550
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:09.760
DINESH MADAVIN: Thank you, Tad.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:10.480
Hi, everyone.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:11.530
I'm Dinesh Madavin.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:13.030
I've been a senior
software engineer

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:16.900
at PayPal doing mostly developer
experience, like ACM, Jenkins,

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:19.330
and deploys.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:22.780
So let's get into
what PayPal is.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:26.340
So this is our PayPal
journey to cloud.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.330
We began way back in
2014 with [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:30.040
to containerize everything.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.750
And we tried a lot of stuff.

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:37.250
While much of it was useful and
made developers' life easier,

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:39.220
the developer experience
team increasingly

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:41.350
struggled with growing
the scale and complexity

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.000
of managing the systems.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.140
This led us directly
to the decision

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:48.040
to migrate everything
to cloud, so that Google

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:49.540
could carry on the
burden of keeping

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:50.930
the infrastructure
healthy and we

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:52.930
could focus on developer
experience, rather than

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:54.370
the infrastructure.

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:56.470
To put this in some
context, in 2013,

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.500
we started the journey with
one monolithic Jenkins,

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:03.700
with hundreds of plugins,
and more than 40,000 jobs,

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.530
running on a DL980.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.770
And this got better after
we migrated to OpenStack

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:13.840
and we used Mesos, Marathon,
and Docker from 2014 to 2016.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.648
And by 2018, we were
creating so many jobs

00:13:17.648 --> 00:13:18.940
that we were not able to scale.

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.490
So we had to--

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:24.220
it was a pain to set up our
own infrastructure, which

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.330
was a bottleneck
for our developers.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.890
So we started migrating
to Google Cloud.

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:33.250
In 2018, we started migrating
all our dev and test

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:36.370
to cloud, which has enabled
us to scale to more than 12

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:38.630
million jobs per year.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:40.000
We are truly scalable now.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:41.840
And this was a huge win for us.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:45.430
And in 2019, we are planning to
migrate our mobile SDLC also,

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:49.480
and use Filestore for
more use cases at PayPal.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:53.822
So to understand why
Filestore is important to us,

00:13:53.822 --> 00:13:55.780
I need to describe our
development architecture

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:56.680
and scale.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:58.180
We'll start with
that, and then dive

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.940
into where Filestore
helps us out.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:02.825
We were early
adopters of Filestore.

00:14:02.825 --> 00:14:04.450
We were amongst the
very first customer

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:07.600
when it launched in beta
back in July of 2018, which

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:10.300
means that we can also cover
our experience with Filestore's

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.023
reliability and availability
during that time.

00:14:13.023 --> 00:14:14.940
Finally, I'll wrap up
by talking about what we

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:19.470
are going to do next and
what kind of platform

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:22.910
we're giving to our developers.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.177
So this is our architecture.

00:14:26.177 --> 00:14:28.010
PayPal's developer
experience is centralized

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.460
around a continuous integration
and continuous deployment

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:32.650
pipeline built on
top of Jenkins,

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:34.170
the leading open
source automation

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:39.120
server, Mesos, Aurora,
Zookeeper, MongoDB, and NGINX.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:40.680
When developer
commits their code,

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:42.700
a build is automatically
kicked off.

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:43.840
Our unit tests are run.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:47.350
And the Docker image that is
built is deployed to a server.

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:50.230
And all the functional
tests are completed,

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:53.140
and it's automatically approved
to be pushed to production.

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:58.920
And this basically happens,
everything in a Jenkins core,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:14:59.560
Jenkins master.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:02.510
That is the core of our
development architecture.

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:04.630
So we make sure each
development team is not

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.870
blocked by any other team by
providing one Jenkins master

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:10.360
for every team that's there.

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:13.660
We also use Mesos to orchestrate
our Jenkins master and slaves,

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:16.900
which enables us to
manage our fleet of GC VMs

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:19.180
as a single pool of
resources that we can

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.060
scale up and down on demand.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.800
Of all this, we also provide
a continuous integration API

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:26.970
which encapsulates our
CI/CD functionality.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:29.530
Our Aurora framework
schedules around running

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.490
process and our Cron jobs.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:34.270
Filestore here provides
persistent storage

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:35.690
for our Jenkins masters.

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:39.180
We are able to scale the
number of Filestore instances

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:42.640
sublinearly with respect to
the number of Jenkins masters,

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:45.400
which is not just a testament
to the Filestore's performance,

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:46.870
but also helps
with manageability

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:49.420
because there are less
resources to keep track of.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:58.140
So before Filestore,
each Jenkins master

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:00.500
also included the CI data.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.250
This means that the
backup has to back up

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:07.090
all the CI data that was inside
the Jenkins master container.

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:09.280
You can recall that we
were well on our way

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:11.770
to 5,000 Jenkins
master containers.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:15.160
And as you can imagine, this
was a huge scalability issue

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.730
for us, and also, it was not as
reliable as we want it to be.

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:22.450
And all the data that was in the
CI we were backing up to GCS,

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:25.630
and learned that encryption,
compression, and network

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:26.900
latency is not your friend.

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:30.560
So during this era
of rapid growth,

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.100
we were in never-ending
cycle of container failures

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:36.460
and also data loss, and
leading to a poor, unproductive

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:37.870
developer experience.

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:40.750
So we stepped back and looked
for alternative solutions.

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:44.260
Naturally, we thought that
making the container stateless

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:45.610
is the way to go.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:49.832
And Filestore was launching
beta at the same time.

00:16:49.832 --> 00:16:51.790
And it turned out that
this was exactly what we

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:55.510
needed to [? refactor ?] the
statefulness of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:03.880
So let's talk about our
use case with Filestore.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:06.250
So what we did was
we were backing up

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:07.839
everything in our containers.

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:11.990
And the containers was
going out of memory.

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:14.440
So we completely
removed the process

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:17.109
of backing up and restoring
from inside the container,

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:19.150
and we moved all our
data to the Filestore.

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:23.109
And we were able to scale our
containers to more than 5,000.

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:26.740
And we were putting it on
Filestore instances mounted

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:28.240
on multiple VMs.

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:32.620
And because we moved our
architecture to the Filestore,

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:37.960
we were able to completely get
rid of the container backups.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:40.030
And after moving
to the new process,

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:42.940
we were able to start up
our containers from-- which

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:45.910
was taking many hours,
sometimes three to four hours,

00:17:45.910 --> 00:17:50.730
to less than a minute,
which was a huge win for us.

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:53.280
So you can recall from
my earlier comments

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.310
that we have been Filestore
customers since beta launch.

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:58.270
And we have seen the
service go from where

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:00.793
we weren't sure we could run
in production to a point where

00:18:00.793 --> 00:18:03.740
we are pleased with this
reliability and availability.

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:06.240
When we migrated to Filestore
last year, we were [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.730
matrix tracked multiple
outages, which sometimes

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:10.560
lasted more than 10 minutes.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.750
And this caused our workloads
to freeze and developments

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:14.250
to stall.

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.440
This means that all
of PayPal could not

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:20.760
run any builds or any deploys,
and everything was frozen.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:23.460
We worked closely with the
Filestore team on these issues.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.650
And over the last six
months, we have not

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:29.490
seen one issue where
Filestore exceeded the SLA.

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:33.210
And this was not even
officially GA yet.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.527
So it is a good thing.

00:18:35.527 --> 00:18:37.110
This significantly
reduced our support

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.660
costs, which is a fancy
way of putting that I'm not

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.197
getting paged anymore.

00:18:41.197 --> 00:18:45.270
[LAUGHS] So the most important
outcome of this thing

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:49.140
is our developers are no longer
experiencing productivity loss

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.960
due to the CI/CD
system being down.

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:56.960
So moving forward, we are
looking at more performance

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:01.460
intensive workloads and
to run our builds also

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:02.600
on the Filestore.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.640
Currently, we are using
a local SSD exported.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.910
We don't expect network
attached storage

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:09.483
to be quite as
fast as the local,

00:19:09.483 --> 00:19:10.900
but we think that
it's fast enough

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:15.130
and the performance trade-off
is worth this simplification

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:17.158
to build this slave environment.

00:19:17.158 --> 00:19:19.200
We have just begun looking
into the restructuring

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:21.517
of our Android and iOS
mobile build and test,

00:19:21.517 --> 00:19:23.600
and we think that we'll
also get the similar level

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:26.940
of maintainability and
scalability our of Filestore.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:28.430
Thanks for listening, everyone.

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:31.040
That's what's up at PayPal.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:33.697
And I'll call Tad
back and hand it off.

