a minimum size of 1 terabyte.

00:34:25.850 --> 00:34:28.727
And then you have your
standard NFS export policy

00:34:28.727 --> 00:34:29.310
permissioning.

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:33.420
I'm going to add a
rule to open all.

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:35.139
I'm very generous
with my security.

00:34:40.239 --> 00:34:41.810
So I open it to all clients.

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.900
And then I can choose
Read and Write.

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:47.110
And with that done, last
thing I'll mention here

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:50.085
before I click Save
is I can now also take

00:34:50.085 --> 00:34:52.210
snapshots in an automated
fashion, where I can then

00:34:52.210 --> 00:34:55.120
set my schedule-- hourly, daily,
weekly, minutes-- and also

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:56.504
my retention.

00:34:56.504 --> 00:34:58.220
I'm not doing that in this case.

00:34:58.220 --> 00:35:00.620
I'll click Save.

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:01.870
As I mentioned, pay attention.

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:03.912
I clicked Save, and it
takes me a couple seconds.

00:35:03.912 --> 00:35:06.130
So we actually-- you
didn't hear this from me--

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:08.530
we have a contest
going on in our booth.

00:35:08.530 --> 00:35:10.270
If you do this and
you do it the fastest,

00:35:10.270 --> 00:35:13.160
then you'll get a
Bose wireless headset.

00:35:13.160 --> 00:35:14.830
You didn't hear
this from me, again.

00:35:14.830 --> 00:35:17.350
Right now, I think the
clock is about 25 seconds.

00:35:17.350 --> 00:35:19.700
And I think you can
easily beat that.

00:35:19.700 --> 00:35:20.930
This is the basic demo.

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:25.010
I'm not going to mount
this volume to an instance,

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:28.550
but I'll show you, if you wanted
to do that, you can quickly

00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:29.873
click on this icon right here.

00:35:29.873 --> 00:35:31.790
And you can check out
your mount instructions.

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:36.710
Simply copy and paste
that in your SSH session

00:35:36.710 --> 00:35:38.360
and you'll be able to do that.

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:41.570
Now, with this basic demo
coming to a conclusion,

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:44.300
I'm going to switch over
to an application demo.

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:51.420
I've kept this
application demo simple.

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:55.620
What I have here is a
Google Cloud Instance,

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:58.362
compute instance, which I
call confluence-next-demo.

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:05.820
And in the next screen, I have
a cloud volume from that app,

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:08.460
also called
confluence-next-data.

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:10.698
And what I'm doing is I'm
installing confluence.

00:36:10.698 --> 00:36:12.990
This is an application example
of an enterprise content

00:36:12.990 --> 00:36:15.600
management system where you're
sharing and collaborating

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:16.410
files.

00:36:16.410 --> 00:36:19.290
So I install that app
on that Google instance,

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:21.930
and then I switch the
directory for data

00:36:21.930 --> 00:36:25.590
to that cloud volume
I just created.

00:36:25.590 --> 00:36:28.570
The app is installed.

00:36:28.570 --> 00:36:32.780
Now what I'll do is I'll fire
it up, create a new workspace,

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:35.760
call it DevOps, and then I'll
add some data, some couple

00:36:35.760 --> 00:36:38.280
of web pages and
some pictures, just

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:39.990
to show you a production state.

00:36:39.990 --> 00:36:44.190
You have some data running
on your application.

00:36:44.190 --> 00:37:00.448
Once this is done, what I'm
going to try to depict here

00:37:00.448 --> 00:37:02.490
is that now you have data
and application running

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:04.573
on Cloud Volumes in Google,
and you're, let's say,

00:37:04.573 --> 00:37:05.490
in production, right?

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:07.530
And maybe you have
a business need.

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:10.260
You want to make some changes
to your steady state and you

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:12.920
want to maybe do an
upgrade or add some--

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:15.030
or maybe move this copy
of your entire application

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:18.340
to another environment,
let's say, PT--

00:37:18.340 --> 00:37:19.388
or something.

00:37:19.388 --> 00:37:21.930
So what you can then do is you
can take an on-demand snapshot

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:24.430
that I showed you in
the UI demo earlier.

00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:28.380
You do that by clicking on
that top left Snapshot icon,

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:31.540
or you can edit the volume.

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:33.460
Once you take the
snapshot, the nice thing

00:37:33.460 --> 00:37:35.140
is that then you can
clone the snapshot

00:37:35.140 --> 00:37:38.410
and create a new volume
from that snapshot.

00:37:41.140 --> 00:37:42.940
This volume that's
been created is now

00:37:42.940 --> 00:37:44.740
going to be also
resized in the process.

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:47.420
So you can change it from
1 terabyte to 2 terabytes.

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:49.810
Now you mount it back to
your confluence instance.

00:37:49.810 --> 00:37:51.540
And you'll notice
that I'll change

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:54.100
the directory for the
data, and I'll still

00:37:54.100 --> 00:37:56.320
have all of my web
pages and the data

00:37:56.320 --> 00:37:59.020
that I created in
the previous step.

00:38:02.017 --> 00:38:03.100
Got a bit ahead of myself.

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:09.260
There it is.

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:12.980
So the new data volume was
mounted, and I have my data.

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:15.500
At the same time, I'm also
migrating my application

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:18.985
from version 6.1 to the
latest version of this app.

00:38:18.985 --> 00:38:20.360
And I can do that
now because I'm

00:38:20.360 --> 00:38:22.230
confident that if
anything goes wrong,

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:24.970
I can go back to that point
in time copy of the snapshot

00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:25.880
that I created.

00:38:32.420 --> 00:38:33.140
That's the demo.

00:38:33.140 --> 00:38:33.640
Thank you.

00:38:33.640 --> 00:38:38.200
Can I switch back
to the PowerPoint?

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:39.022
Next slide.

00:38:39.022 --> 00:38:39.730
I should do that.

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:47.520
OK, so just to--

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:50.568
on a closing note, the service
is currently in paid alpha.

00:38:50.568 --> 00:38:53.110
So you have to be whitelisted
in order to access the service.

00:38:53.110 --> 00:38:56.200
But that's changing next month
when we go to beta release.

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:59.230
A couple of new features that
are coming out with beta--

00:38:59.230 --> 00:39:00.610
we'll be exposing our API.

00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:03.670
So now you can take that API and
programmatically access Cloud

00:39:03.670 --> 00:39:06.040
Volumes with other
Google technologies,

00:39:06.040 --> 00:39:10.300
and provision that from your
Google Cloud command line

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:11.380
tools.

00:39:11.380 --> 00:39:13.657
And the other cool feature
that's coming out in beta

00:39:13.657 --> 00:39:16.240
is that we'll be automating the
entire process of whitelisting

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:20.290
and onboarding, so you can
now, in a self-service fashion,

00:39:20.290 --> 00:39:23.570
access to services open to
all, basically, next month.

00:39:23.570 --> 00:39:25.240
From a regional
standpoint, currently we

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:27.670
are available in central-1
and us-east4 regions.

00:39:27.670 --> 00:39:31.090
But we'll be adding
us-west1, which is LA,

00:39:31.090 --> 00:39:34.360
and eu-west2, which I
believe is Frankfurt,

00:39:34.360 --> 00:39:36.140
in the summer time frame.

00:39:36.140 --> 00:39:39.970
And then following that
would be London and Sydney.

00:39:39.970 --> 00:39:41.678
I think I'll stop
with that, Tad.

00:39:41.678 --> 00:39:42.970
So thank you all for listening.

00:39:42.970 --> 00:39:46.000
And back to you.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:46.720
TAD HUNT: Great.

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:47.390
Awesome demo.

00:39:47.390 --> 00:39:48.020
Thanks, Niraj.

00:39:48.020 --> 00:39:50.120
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:50.120 --> 00:39:50.620
Cool.

00:39:50.620 --> 00:39:53.162
So that was a great demo of some
key enterprise collaboration

00:39:53.162 --> 00:39:57.820
workflow on GCP, and of course,
the UI for the Cloud Volumes

00:39:57.820 --> 00:39:58.780
Service.

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:01.330
So this is at the
wrap-up section,

00:40:01.330 --> 00:40:03.700
so I'm just going to
summarize what we covered.

00:40:03.700 --> 00:40:05.710
We covered Cloud
Filestore's ability

00:40:05.710 --> 00:40:08.320
to do some general
purpose NAS and drill down

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:10.240
into the dev test area.

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:13.870
We looked at Elastifile's
GKE and preemptable VM story

00:40:13.870 --> 00:40:15.340
for large scale.

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:18.838
And we looked at NetApps
Enterprise Content Management.

00:40:18.838 --> 00:40:20.380
So I think the really
cool thing here

00:40:20.380 --> 00:40:22.930
is you see three different
managed services all built

00:40:22.930 --> 00:40:24.250
on GCP.

00:40:24.250 --> 00:40:27.100
And what we talked about at
our keynote this morning,

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:31.060
the open source
services platform,

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:32.590
and bringing open source in.

00:40:32.590 --> 00:40:34.090
We're actually doing
the same thing.

00:40:34.090 --> 00:40:39.310
And we've really prototyped
that these industry partners

00:40:39.310 --> 00:40:40.735
as well.

00:40:40.735 --> 00:40:42.610
So of course, there's
a lot of more workloads

00:40:42.610 --> 00:40:46.030
that all of our partners
in Filestore can do.

00:40:46.030 --> 00:40:49.840
So please check out
these folks' websites,

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:52.090
some of the other
sessions we talked about,

00:40:52.090 --> 00:40:54.080
and everything else out there.

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:57.400
The key takeaway here is, if you
walk away with only one thing,

00:40:57.400 --> 00:41:00.910
is Google Cloud is actually
very ready for your enterprise

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:01.600
workloads.

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:02.500
Bring them on.

